Subject: [PATCH] dwc2: kick the root-hub status poll on port interrupts
Upstream-Status: Pending

Second car capture with 0046 v3 on the NAC Wave 2, two role switches,
both lost the head unit before a bus reset was ever asserted:

  [ 1.082453] Port Connect Detected
  [ 1.336323] hprt0 sample 2/24: HPRT0=00021000 [PWR ]    <- gone, no reset
  [ 9.002544] Port Connect Detected
  [ 9.253253] hprt0 sample 2/24: HPRT0=00021000 [PWR ]    <- gone, no reset

In the capture before that the second switch succeeded, with the hub
thread starting the reset 122ms after the connect. Same kernel, same
head unit, idle system at 9s: the time from connect to reset varies
from ~120ms to more than 250ms.

That variance is usbcore's root-hub polling. dwc2 does not set
uses_new_polling and never calls usb_hcd_poll_rh_status() from its
port interrupt, so the root hub's status URB is only completed by
rh_timer, which fires every 250ms, aligned to jiffies/(HZ/4)
("The USB 2.0 spec says 256 ms. This is close enough", hcd.c). A port
change is noticed with a uniformly random 0-250ms delay before
hub_port_connect() even runs - and then patch 0047's connect debounce
(~100-125ms) came on top. A head unit that swapped roles on our
request sits as a device waiting for that reset and gives up after
~200ms. With those two delays summing to 100-375ms, the role switch
worked about one time in four. ludwig-v's number in catplay-firmware
issue #1 was one in four.

Do what ehci, ohci and musb do: call usb_hcd_poll_rh_status() when the
port interrupt fired, after hsotg->lock is released. The root-hub URB
completes immediately, hub_irq() kicks the hub workqueue, and with 0047
the reset follows within a few ms of "Port Connect Detected". Cheap and
correct on every platform; here it is the difference between a race
and a handshake.

Written against the letux-6.18.y tree at a3d00c045d89 with 0022
applied. Not yet compiled or run.
---
 drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_intr.c | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_intr.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_intr.c
@@ -2203,6 +2203,7 @@
 {
 	u32 gintsts, dbg_gintsts;
 	irqreturn_t retval = IRQ_HANDLED;
+	bool port_change = false;
 
 	if (!dwc2_is_controller_alive(hsotg)) {
 		dev_warn(hsotg->dev, "Controller is dead\n");
@@ -2243,8 +2244,10 @@
 			dwc2_rx_fifo_level_intr(hsotg);
 		if (gintsts & GINTSTS_NPTXFEMP)
 			dwc2_np_tx_fifo_empty_intr(hsotg);
-		if (gintsts & GINTSTS_PRTINT)
+		if (gintsts & GINTSTS_PRTINT) {
 			dwc2_port_intr(hsotg);
+			port_change = true;
+		}
 		if (gintsts & GINTSTS_HCHINT)
 			dwc2_hc_intr(hsotg);
 		if (gintsts & GINTSTS_PTXFEMP)
@@ -2262,5 +2265,19 @@
 
 	spin_unlock(&hsotg->lock);
 
+	/*
+	 * Hand root-hub port changes to usbcore now rather than leaving them
+	 * for its 250ms status poll. Without this, the time from "Port
+	 * Connect Detected" to the hub thread even looking at the port is
+	 * uniformly random between 0 and 250ms, since dwc2 does not use
+	 * usb_hcd_poll_rh_status() the way ehci/ohci/musb do on a port
+	 * change. Cheap at every other HCD, fatal here: a head unit that has
+	 * just swapped roles into device mode waits only ~200ms for the bus
+	 * reset before it gives up. Called outside hsotg->lock: the root-hub
+	 * URB completion runs hub_irq(), which does not re-enter the HCD.
+	 */
+	if (port_change)
+		usb_hcd_poll_rh_status(dwc2_hsotg_to_hcd(hsotg));
+
 	return retval;
 }
